WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.733 --> 00:00:01.901 line:0%
-No. I'm aware.

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:05.104 line:0%
The thing is that the car
was for my daughter.

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:07.640 line:0%
And she changed her mind.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.742 line:0%
She doesn't want the car.

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.844 line:0%
Yeah. I know.

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:15.047
Anyway, yeah.
Well, it's a long story.

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:18.318
But I think we need to talk
about the $5,000 deposit.

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.789
I understand your policy, sir.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:26.359
But you got to have
some flexibility here.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:31.431
Well, for instance, how about,
you know, 50% back?

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.032
-Dad?

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:34.934
-Okay. How about 40%.

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:39.105
40%, You keep $3,000.
-Dad! [ Bleep ]

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.074
-Let me put you on hold
for a second.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.779
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Sean Penn.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:49.649
This is very serious movie.
It's a very dramatic film.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.418
There are nice funny
father-daughter moments

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:55.722
in the film, especially
about the idea of honesty.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:57.957
And there is a really funny
scene you talk about

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.426
smoking weed,
the two characters.

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:02.261
And I'm wondering,
did that give you flash backs

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:05.298
to the actual moments
with Dylan?

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.500
And I'm wondering
were you the kind of parent that

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.303
was honest with your kid
or were you a liar?

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.772
-Yeah. Well, this is an
interesting thing that we do.

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:17.043
What we convince ourselves of
as honesty

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:20.479
and those things that might
just be adult privacies.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.015
-Right. Right.
-And kids are so --

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:27.186
All kids have such an intuitive
sense of what's authentic

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:33.660
and are all driven to know
their parents' authentic self.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.396
And I think, you know,
I can even say that

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:41.533
while we were making this movie,
still an evolving thing

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:42.835
'cause it's like
peeling an onion

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:45.638
to offer your authentic self.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:48.775
And there was so much
in Jez Butterworth's writing

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.344
that could parallel things.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:55.948
Not marijuana because I wasn't
ever -- I couldn't take pot.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:59.485
I'd just hear police sirens
and hide in a bathroom.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.355
-[ Laughs ] It is a paranoid
experience, yeah.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:06.726
-But there were
plenty of other areas

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:10.330
where, you know, you find
that you were less than honest.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:15.300
And so these --
I love in this case the extreme

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.106
of what
corrosive deception does.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:24.510
And I felt also, of course,
thematically that part

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.046
of the theme was something we
all are living with

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:30.516
in such a loud and present way
now.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.785
-It is really --
I think you're right.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.421
Like, as long as you can be
authentic about it,

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.556
whether or not it's the whole
truth, it doesn't matter.

00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:39.525
Your kids don't actually care
about the truth

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:41.427
or the lie about the fact.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.561
They just want to know, like,

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:44.464
are you telling me
something you believe?

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.600
-Yeah. Who are you?
-Yeah.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.236
There is a lot of -- Music
is a big part of this film.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.305
I feel like historically it's
always a big part of your films.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:53.373
-Yeah.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.409
-When you're trying
to find a song,

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.444
like, what sends you
on that journey?

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.646
Is it something in the scene

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:01.947
that you feel
like you need the music for?

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.349
-Well, typically --

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.952
if it's something
I have written from go,

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.487
whether adapted or original,

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:13.559
I have found that I involve
songs as characters.

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.494
Music as characters.

00:03:15.494 --> 00:03:17.763
And then with
something like this

00:03:17.763 --> 00:03:19.632
that I didn't write
the original script on,

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:23.269
I did revisions by making
edits where I felt

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.571
that I could tell something
with images and music

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:27.873
that was otherwise written
as a scene.

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:35.515
So the -- And I had such a great
collaboration with Eddie Vedder

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:39.585
on another movie and on
"Into the Wild,"

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:42.689
and he came onboard on this
and really kind of curated

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:46.626
all the rest of it, Cat Power
and Glen Hansard

00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:48.927
and then Olivia Vedder,

00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:50.796
Eddie's teenage daughter,

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:53.866
beautifully sang
the end two songs.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:58.036
But, yes, I always love that
kind of cinema starting back

00:03:58.036 --> 00:04:00.005
whether it is Mike Nichols
with "The Graduate"

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:03.743
or "Harold and Maude,"

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:07.680
where, you know,
songs could help tell the story

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.583
and I enjoy watching a movie
and feeling that music with it.

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:14.554
-You were on about a year ago.
We were talking remotely.

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:17.356
But we were talking
about your CORE foundation,

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:19.525
which has obviously done
great work internationally.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:24.664
But you're using it domestically
to do a lot of vaccine outreach.

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.099
Progress?

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.833
Do you feel like things are
moving in the right direction?

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:30.236
How is it going?
You're in California a lot.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:32.104
-Yeah. Well, we -- You know,

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:33.606
we're spread out
across the states.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:39.812
We're in India, we're in Brazil,
we're in Haiti, vaccinating now.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.614
You know, started with testing.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.715
There was -- As we know, I mean,

00:04:43.715 --> 00:04:46.151
it's not a story
I can only tell.

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:49.355
It was -- There was a lot of
progress early on.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:56.029
That people have invested in
a very fraudulent idea

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:58.864
of individuality and freedom

00:04:58.864 --> 00:05:04.570
and attached that to denying
basic science is disheartening.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.107
That's what I call kind of a
radical Libertarianism

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:11.544
that is a spinoff of an identity
that is going to hurt their kids

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:12.912
in the long run.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:15.948
And so we're up against it
that way.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:18.216
We're trying to sensitize
other neighborhoods.

00:05:18.216 --> 00:05:21.120
The there are people
who are of good will who are --

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.121
have been afraid of it.

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.056
You talked about the FDA today.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:25.158
I think that's very important.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:27.125
It's also going to give
businesses cover

00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:30.662
to start mandating it,
which I believe is, you know,

00:05:30.662 --> 00:05:32.064
it's like a bad word,

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.267
but it actually means you have
to turn your headlights on

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.002
when you're driving
down the freeway

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:37.870
at night.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.909
So there is progress,
and there are setbacks.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:45.778
Case in point,
we had progress moving

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.479
on the vaccinating in Haiti

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:51.084
and then
the president was assassinated

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:53.986
followed up immediately by
another devastating earthquake.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:57.556
But we put one foot in front
of the other and keep on going.

00:05:57.556 --> 00:05:59.759
-I know you're heading down
there right after this.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.728
Thanks again for continuing to
put your efforts to good use.

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:04.530
And it's just lovely
talking to you as always.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.965
-Great to talk you to.
-Thanks for being here.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:09.368
Please visit
www.coreresponse.org

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:10.336
for more information.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:11.371
"Flag Day" is in theaters now.

